  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/7/7c/Daredevil_Vol_1_1.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A game of poker is interrupted by a yellow-and-red costumed fighter who crashes the scene, demanding that they tell him where he can find \"the Fixer.\"&nbsp; He proceeds to demonstrate that he's a force to be reckoned with before finally introducing himself as Daredevil.\n\nThe scene then shifts to a flashback to 1950, where 8-year-old Matt Murdock is being told by his father \"Battling Murdock\" how important it is to study.&nbsp; He promised his mother before she died that he wouldn't let their son \"grow up to be an uneducated pug like me!\"&nbsp; He wants better things for his son, even though Matt seems proud of his father.&nbsp; But the dad is past his prime now.\n\nAnd so Matt becomes the top student in his class, forsaking all sports, even though he longs to participate.&nbsp; His constant studies gain him the nickname \"Daredevil\" by the crueler kids who laugh at him and think he's a sissy.&nbsp; So he begins to train in the privacy of his home using his dad's many exercise machines and equipment.&nbsp; But he never forgets the ultimate goal - to be the best student he can.&nbsp; His dad is thrilled and tells him \"the day will come when you'll <i>thank</i> me, boy!&nbsp; You're gonna <i>amount</i> to something...just the way your mother would've <i>wanted</i> you to!\"\n\nBut the dad is haunted by his advancing age, hoping he'll get enough work - to keep fighting until his son can get through college.&nbsp; \"I <i>owe</i> him that...for the way he's worked all these years!\"&nbsp; But as he's now \"washed up,\" he has no choice but to turn to someone like the \"Fixer\" - someone he's always sworn he'd steer clear of.&nbsp; But he has no choice.&nbsp; \"I <i>have</i> to get a fight!\"\n\nHe reluctantly signs a contract with the Fixer, but when he returns home to tell Matt that he'll now be able to send him to college, Matt is not there.&nbsp; Matt is returning from the library and sees a blind man trying to cross the street and moves to help, \"...taking the most important few steps of his entire life!\"&nbsp; He sees a truck (labeled \"Ajax Atomic Labs Radio-Active Materials\") barreling toward the man.&nbsp; He jumps into action and is able to throw the man out of the truck's path, but he himself is hit, and \"a cylinder fell from the truck...it struck his face!\"\n\nMatt has lost his sight, but the doctors have hope that an operation might one day restore it.&nbsp; In the meantime, he promises his dad he will continue his studies and get his diploma.&nbsp; But he soon finds that...\"Ever since my accident, I seem able to do everything lots better than before...even without my sight!&nbsp; It's as though nature made all my senses far more powerful, to compensate for my blindness!\"&nbsp; It must have been the radioactive materials in the truck!\n\nAnd soon, as he promised, he graduates from high school and proceeds onto college, sharing a dorm room with a new friend, Franklin \"Foggy\" Nelson.&nbsp; His studies now seem even easier than ever.&nbsp; \"<i>Everything</i> seems easy for me now!&nbsp; All my senses are razor sharp!\"&nbsp; (The story then highlights what he can do with his 4 remaining senses.)&nbsp; But his most important new ability is what appears to be a \"radar\" sense that allows him to navigate everything without bumping into things.\n\nAll the while, Battling Murdock seems to have come upon a stroke of good fortune, winning fights right and left.&nbsp; But he has no idea that it's all because the Fixer has set up every one of them, paying his opponents to \"take a dive.\"&nbsp; All this to build him up...but the day of reckoning is coming now!&nbsp; Soon Foggy approaches Matt on campus, excited to see in the newspaper that Murdock is set to face Davis, the #2 contender.&nbsp; Matt bought tickets to the bout already.&nbsp; But Battling Murdock has been told to take a dive in the first round that night.\n\nHowever, Murdock sees his son in the crowd, there to root for him.&nbsp; He refuses to disappoint him.&nbsp; And he begins the fight of his life, while from the sideline he hears the Fixer calling for him to go down, but he refuses!&nbsp; \"It's my one chance!&nbsp; Maybe my last chance...to do something to make my son <i>proud</i> of me!&nbsp; I'm not gonna fail him!&nbsp; I'm gonna win!\"&nbsp; <b>And he does!</b>\n\nIn the dressing room afterward, Matt and Foggy congratulate Murdock.&nbsp; \"I wanted you to be proud of me, Matt...my son!\"&nbsp; But the Fixer is nearby in a dark sedan, seething about the double-cross.&nbsp; And moments later, outside the gym - <i>CRACK!</i>  Murdock has had his last triumph!\n\nMatt grieves for his father, but he'll be graduating soon, and Foggy wants them to be partners via an office that his wealthy father is setting up.&nbsp; \"Nelson and Murdock, Attorneys at Law\" is born!&nbsp; Their new secretary is Karen Page, and Matt is stricken with her right away.&nbsp; (We find out later in the issue that she's a stranger to New York and so she's able to spend her free time at the office.&nbsp; And she even says to Foggy later, about Matt - \"he seems to need someone to look after him!\")\n\nBut his dad's murder remains foremost on his mind.&nbsp; But he promised his dad years ago that he would use his <i>head</i> and never become a fighter, depending only on his strength.&nbsp; But his new abilities surely would be of some aid, so he decides to create an alter-ego to allow Matt Murdock to remain true to his promise.&nbsp; He creates a costume and decides to use the name the kids on his block used to deride him with - Daredevil!&nbsp; And a modified version of his cane will be his weapon (which he later refers to as a \"billy club\")!\n\nHe starts out by tracking down his father's manager - the \"Fixer.\"&nbsp; He confronts the Fixer who tells his boys to attack, and we soon watch as he navigates through them using his enhanced senses.&nbsp; With his boys down, he orders the Fixer to tell him if he had anything to do with Murdock's death - and the change in his pulse rate indicates he's lying about it.\n\nBut they manage to briefly get away, except on the street below, now back as the blind Matt Murdock, he's easily able to follow the smell of the Fixer's cigar down the road.&nbsp; They make it to the subway, where they plan to slip away, but they discover Daredevil now blocking their path.&nbsp; DD takes down Slade, the guy who actually pulled the trigger in his father's death, but as he pursues the Fixer, the latter suffers from a sudden heart attack and dies.&nbsp; The police arrive and haul off Slade after DD manipulates him into confessing.\n\nAs Matt arrives back at his office, he finds that an accused murderer named Slade has called and asked them to defend him!&nbsp; But Foggy turned him down, convinced from the police report and he is guilty.&nbsp; He hopes Matt doesn't mind!\n\nMatt stands by the window - \"Dad, wherever you are...I kinda hope you're resting easier now!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "there's not much to find fault with here.&nbsp; From start to finish, this book, both the current events and the flashback are about as compelling an origin story as has been pulled off by Marvel to this point.\n\nThe character of Battling Murdock (I don't recall reading the name \"Jack\" here, but perhaps I simply missed it in the text) is completely believable, wanting his son to amount to more than the uneducated fighter he'd become.&nbsp; Matt's story too is largely relatable, a boy who wanted to make his father proud of him and did the right thing, putting his studies first while at the same time taking advantage of the equipment around him to hone his physical skills as well.&nbsp; The introductions of Foggy at college and later Karen at the office were mostly well done (though the attraction between Matt and Karen could have waited an issue or two to let it develop a bit more organically).\n\nBut by far the most emotional part of the story was that of Battling \"Kid\" Murdock going against the Fixer's wishes to make his boy proud of him and the price he paid for that one brief moment of triumph.&nbsp; It wasn't quite the gut-punch of seeing Peter Parker come face-to-face with his uncle's murderer, since the older Murdock <i>had</i> to have known that there would be a price to pay...and did it <i>anyway</i> for the chance to witness the look on his son's face.&nbsp; Even so, <i>powerful</i> stuff here!\n\nMy first exposure to this origin story was in the Roger McKenzie/Frank Miller re-telling in <i>Daredevil</i> #164, which was published soon after I began collecting comics, and I had long assumed they had added many little \"extras\" to the tale, as we'd seen done with the Spider-Man origin.&nbsp; And while that is certainly still true, I found the original version stocked with a wealth of detail and emotional impact that I had long assumed absent due to the lack-of-space problems that seemed to plague most Silver Age origin stories - this one felt far more <i>complete</i> than any previous one.\n\nIf there are any negatives at all to be found, it's the unfortunate re-use of radioactivity as the cause of his new abilities (which was brushed over so quickly that we almost forget about it), but more importantly - that horrible costume!&nbsp; Yes, it was designed by a blind man, so the lack of fashion can thus be easily explained away, but the creators of the magazine surely must be blamed instead!&nbsp; Thankfully it took only 6 issues before that horrible ketchup and mustard version was retired in favor of his more familiar all-red version in issue 7.&nbsp; That and the needlessly immediate chemistry between Karen and Matt prevent this one from reaching full NM status.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe novelty of a blind superhero and the small explanations in the thought bubbles during the action which explain how he's navigating the things around him <i>definitely</i> are compelling enough to push this to the brink of NM.&nbsp; I still think the story within [LINK:1963:ASM_10|ASM #10|2027-04-05] is better overall than this tale, but the story and origin aren't the only factors - the <i>cumulative</i> effect of the interesting new character and origin story push this one slightly ahead of virtually everything that Marvel has published thus far, and I award it almost full marks.&nbsp; <i>Well done!</i>\n\nSide Notes:\nIn 1964, we can assume Matt is 22 years-old (having been 8 in 1950, as indicated in the text).&nbsp; Yeah, it seems highly unlikely that an accused murderer would immediately give a brand-new law firm the call for legal defense, with two lawyers fresh out of college, so that too pulls this one down from NM status.\n\nThis is the most careful reading I've even given to the origin of Daredevil, and while I know the story behind Battling Murdock is well-documented, I do now wonder whether Marvel ever plumbed the depths of his mother.&nbsp; I suppose I'll just keep reading and find out!\n\nI would be totally remiss if I did not at least acknowledge the art of Bill Everett, who only did this one issue before realizing that there was no way he could commit to it on a regular basis (the <i>Announcements</i> section of FF #27 explain more about why).&nbsp; Obviously a veteran of comics, credited with the creation of the Golden Age Sub-Mariner, I thought his artwork was quite satisfactory and captured the motion of the action sequences well, and his other figures were good too and in no way distracted from the story.&nbsp; I was sad when I learned as a result of this effort on my part that he only did this one issue.&nbsp; But I know he'll eventually be back on the Sub-Mariner later on, so good for him!",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}